"Introducing IndQA | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/introducing-indqa)\nciteturn2095view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openai.com/index/introducing-indqa\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://openai.com/index/introducing-indqa/; Total lines: 212\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: November 3, 2025\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Research cite4†Release L16: # Introducing IndQA\nL17: \nL18: A new benchmark for evaluating AI systems on Indian culture and languages.\nL19: \nL20: Loading…\nL21: \nL22: Share\nL23: \nL24: How it works\nL25: \nL26:   * cite5†How it works L27:   * cite6†How we built IndQA L28:   * cite7†Example questions L29:   * cite8†Improvements over time L30:     * cite9†Caveats L31:   * cite10†The experts behind IndQA L32:   * cite11†Next steps L33: \nL34:   * cite5†How it works L35:   * cite6†How we built IndQA L36:   * cite7†Example questions L37:   * cite8†Improvements over time L38:     * cite9†Caveats L39:   * cite10†The experts behind IndQA L40:   * cite11†Next steps L41: Our mission is to make AGI benefit all of humanity. If AI is going to be useful for everyone, it needs to work well across languages and cultures. About 80 percent of people worldwide do not speak English as their primary language, yet most existing benchmarks that measure non-English language capabilities fall short.\nL42: Existing multilingual benchmarks like cite12†MMMLU⁠(opens in a new window)†huggingface.co are now saturated—top models cluster near high scores—which make them less useful for measuring real progress. In addition, current benchmarks mostly focus on translation or multiple-choice tasks. They don’t adequately capture what really matters for evaluating an AI system’s language capabilities—understanding context, culture, history, and the things that matter to people where they live.\nL43: That’s why we built IndQA, a new benchmark designed to evaluate how well AI models understand and reason about questions that matter in Indian languages, across a wide range of cultural domains. While our aim is to create similar benchmarks for other languages and regions, India is an obvious starting point.\nL44: India has about a billion people who don’t use English as their primary language, 22 official languages (including at least seven with over 50 million speakers), and is ChatGPT’s second largest market.\nL45: This work is part of our ongoing commitment to improve our products and tools for Indian users, and to make our technology more accessible throughout the country.\nL46: ## How it works\nL47: \nL48: IndQA evaluates knowledge and reasoning about Indian culture and everyday life in Indian languages. It spans 2,278 questions across 12 languages and 10 cultural domains, created in partnership with 261 domain experts from across India. Unlike existing benchmarks like MMMLU and MGSM, it is designed to probe culturally nuanced, reasoning-heavy tasks that existing evaluations struggle to capture.\nL49: IndQA covers a broad range of culturally relevant topics, such as Architecture & Design, Arts & Culture, Everyday Life, Food & Cuisine, History, Law & Ethics, Literature & Linguistics, Media & Entertainment, Religion & Spirituality, and Sports & Recreation—with items written natively in Bengali, English, Hindi, Hinglish, Kannada, Marathi, Odia, Telugu, Gujarati, Malayalam, Punjabi, and Tamil. Note: We specifically added Hinglish given the prevalence of code-switching in conversations.\nL50: Each datapoint includes a culturally grounded prompt in an Indian language, an English translation for auditability, rubric criteria for grading, and an ideal answer that reflects expert expectations.\nL51: IndQA uses a rubric-based approach. Each response is graded against criteria written by domain experts for that specific question. The criteria spell out what an ideal answer should include or avoid, and each one is given a weighted point value based on its importance. A model-based grader checks whether each criterion is met. The final score is the sum of the points for criteria satisfied out of the total possible.\nL52: ## How we built IndQA\nL53: \nL54:   * Expert‑authored questions. We worked with partners to find experts in India across 10 different domains. They drafted difficult, reasoning‑focused prompts tied to their regions and specialties. These experts are native‑level speakers of the relevant language (and English) and bring deep subject expertise.\nL55:   * Adversarial filtering: Each question was tested against OpenAI’s strongest models at the time of their creation: GPT‑4o, OpenAI o3, GPT‑4.5, and (partially, post public launch) GPT‑5. We kept only those questions where a majority of these models failed to produce acceptable answers, preserving headroom for progress\nL56:   * Detailed Criteria. Along with every question, domain experts provided criteria used to grade the model response, similar to an exam rubric for an essay question. These criteria are used to grade responses from candidate models.\nL57: \nL58:   * Ideal answers + review. Experts added ideal answers and English translations, followed by peer review and iterative fixes until sign‑off.\nL59: ## Example questions\nL60: \nL61: Bengali Gujarati Hindi Hinglish Kannada Malayalam Marathi Odia Punjabi Tamil Telugu\nL62: ## Improvements over time\nL63: \nL64: We use IndQA to evaluate how recent frontier models perform and chart progress over the last couple years. With IndQA we can see that OpenAI’s models have improved significantly over time on Indian languages (with cite9†caveats⁠ ), but still have substantial room for improvement. We look forward to improving performance and sharing results for future models.\nL65: \nL66: We also stratify performance on IndQA by Language and Domain below, comparing GPT‑5 Thinking High to other frontier models.\nL67: ### Caveats\nL68: \nL69: Because questions are not identical across languages, IndQA is not a language leaderboard; cross‑language scores shouldn’t be interpreted as direct comparisons of language ability. Instead, we plan to use IndQA to measure improvement over time within a model family or configuration.\nL70: Additionally, because questions were filtered to those GPT‑4o, OpenAI o3, GPT‑4.5, and (post public launch) GPT‑5 could not answer sufficiently, question selection is adversarial against these models. This potentially confounds the relative performance of GPT‑5, and could disadvantage all OpenAI models compared to non-OpenAI models.\nL71: ## The experts behind IndQA\nL72: \nL73: We’re grateful to the 261 Indian experts—journalists, linguists, scholars, artists, and industry practitioners—who authored and reviewed questions for IndQA. A few examples of the experts we worked with includes:\nL74: \nL75:   * A Nandi Award winning Telugu actor and screenwriter with over 750 films\nL76: \nL77:   * A Marathi journalist and editor at Tarun Bharat\nL78: \nL79:   * A scholar of Kannada linguistics and dictionary editor\nL80: \nL81:   * An International Chess Grandmaster who coaches top-100 chess players\nL82:   * A Tamil writer, poet, and cultural activist advocating for social justice, caste equity, and literary freedom\nL83: \nL84:   * An award winning Punjabi music composer\nL85: \nL86:   * A Gujarati heritage curator and conservation specialist\nL87: \nL88:   * An award winning Malayalam poet and performance artist\nL89: \nL90:   * A professor of history, specializing in Bengal's rich cultural heritage\nL91: \nL92:   * A professor of architecture, focusing on Odishan temples\nL93: ## Next steps\nL94: \nL95: We hope the release of IndQA will inform and inspire new benchmark creation from the research community. IndQA style questions are especially valuable in languages or cultural domains that are poorly covered by existing AI benchmarks. Creating similar benchmarks to IndQA can help AI research labs learn more about languages and domains models struggle with today, and provide a north star for improvements in the future.\nL96: \nL97:   * cite13†2025 L98:   * cite14†Language L99:   * cite15†Reasonings & Policy L100: ## Author\nL101: \nL102: OpenAI\nL103: ## Keep reading\nL104: \nL105: cite16†View all L106: \nL107: cite17†Introducing GPT-6.1 Sol ProductSep 29, 2026 L108: \nL109: cite18†Introducing GPT-6 Sol and Luna ProductSep 22, 2026 L110: \nL111: cite19†Our framework for reporting model misalignment ResearchSep 16, 2026 L112: \nL113: Research\nL114: \nL115:   * cite20†Research Index L116:   * cite21†Research Overview L117:   * cite22†Economic Research L118: \nL119: Latest Advancements\nL120: \nL121:   * cite17†GPT-6.1 Sol L122:   * cite23†GPT-6 Astra L123:   * cite24†GPT-5.6 L124:   * cite25†GPT-5.5 L125: \nL126: Safety\nL127:   * cite26†Safety Approach L128:   * cite27†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L129:   * cite28†Security & Privacy L130:   * cite29†Trust & Transparency L131: \nL132: Products\nL133:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L134:   * cite30†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L135:   * cite31†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L136:   * cite32†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L137:   * cite33†Codex†chatgpt.com L138:   * cite34†Dots(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L139:   * cite35†Release Notes L140: \nL141: API Platform\nL142: \nL143:   * cite36†Overview L144:   * cite37†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L145:   * cite38†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L146: \nL147: Business\nL148:   * cite39†Overview L149:   * cite40†Solutions L150:   * cite41†Resources L151:   * cite42†Plugins L152:   * cite43†Customer Stories L153:   * cite44†Partner Network L154:   * cite45†Contact Sales L155: \nL156: Developers\nL157: \nL158:   * cite46†Apps SDK(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L159:   * cite47†Open Models L160:   * cite48†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L161:   * cite49†Resources(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L162:   * cite50†Developer Forum(opens in a new window)†community.openai.com L163: \nL164: Company\nL165:   * cite51†About Us L166:   * cite52†Our Charter L167:   * cite53†Careers L168:   * cite16†News L169: \nL170: Support\nL171: \nL172:   * cite54†Help Center(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L173: \nL174: More\nL175: \nL176:   * cite55†Stories L177:   * cite56†Academy L178:   * cite57†Supply Co. L179:   * cite58†Livestreams L180:   * cite59†Podcast L181:   * cite60†RSS L182: \nL183: Terms & Policies\nL184: \nL185:   * cite61†Terms of Use L186:   * cite62†Privacy Policy L187:   * cite63†Other Policies L188: cite64†(opens in a new window)†x.com cite65†(opens in a new window)†www.youtube.com cite66†(opens in a new window)†www.linkedin.com cite67†(opens in a new window)†github.com cite68†(opens in a new window)†www.instagram.com cite69†(opens in a new window)†www.tiktok.com cite70†(opens in a new window)†discord.gg L189: \nL190: OpenAI © 2015–2026 Your privacy choices\nL191: \nL192: English United States\nL193: \nL194: [Input: Search]\nL195: ## Language: Bengali\nL196: ## Domain: Literature and linguistics\nL197: \nL198: Prompt\nL199: \nL200: ‘দণ্ডক থেকে মরিচঝাঁপি’ উপন্যাসের লেখক নিম্নবর্ণের পুরুষ ও নারীদের দণ্ডকারন্যে পুনর্বাসন পরবর্তী জীবন কিভাবে দেখিয়েছেন? দণ্ডকারণ্যে পুনর্বাসন কি সরকারী উদাসীনতার ফল? পরিবর্তিত প্রাকৃতিক পরিবেশের সাথে উদ্বাস্তুরা কিভাবে মানিয়ে নিয়েছিল?\nL201: \nL202: English Translation\nL203: How did the writer of Bengali novel ‘Dandak Theke Marichjhanpi’ depict the post-rehabilitation lives of lower caste men and women? Was the rehabilitation in Dandakaranya a result of governmental indifference? What was its relation with the new natural landscapes?\nL204: ## Domain: Food and cuisine\nL205: \nL206: Prompt\nL207: \nL208: কোন পরিপ্রেক্ষিতে উনিশ শতকের শেষ দিক থেকে রান্নার বইগুলো বেরচ্ছিল ? প্রথম বাংলা রান্নার বইটির সাথে বিপ্রদাস মুখোপাধ্যায় রচিত বইটির পার্থক্য কোথায় ? বিপ্রদাসের উদ্যোগে প্রকাশিত পত্রিকাটি চলেছিল কতদিন ? বিপ্রদাস ও প্রজ্ঞা সুন্দরীর লেখা অনুসরণ করে দিঘাপতিয়া থেকে কোন বইটি বেরিয়েছিল ?\nL209: \nL210: English Translation\nL211: In what context were cookbooks published from the end of the 19th century? What is the difference between the first Bengali cookbook and the book written by Bipradas Mukherjee? How long did the magazine published by Bipradas run? Which book was published by Dighapatiya following the writings of Bipradas and Pragya Sundari?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAWS and OpenAI announce multi-year strategic partnership | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/aws-and-openai-partnership)\nciteturn2095view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openai.com/index/aws-and-openai-partnership\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://openai.com/index/aws-and-openai-partnership/; Total lines: 138\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: November 3, 2025\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Company L16: # AWS and OpenAI announce multi-year strategic partnership\nL17: \nL18: Partnership will enable OpenAI to run its advanced AI workloads on AWS’s world-class infrastructure starting immediately.\nL19: \nL20: Loading…\nL21: \nL22: Share\nL23: \nL24: Key takeaways:\nL25: \nL26:   * The multi-year, strategic partnership empowers OpenAI with immediate and increasing access to AWS’s world-class infrastructure for their advanced AI workloads.\nL27:   * AWS to provide OpenAI with Amazon EC2 UltraServers, featuring hundreds of thousands of chips, and the ability to scale to tens of millions of CPUs for its advanced generative AI workloads.\nL28: \nL29:   * Representing a $38B commitment, OpenAI will rapidly expand compute capacity while benefitting from the price, performance, scale, and security of AWS.\nL30: Today, Amazon Web Services (AWS) and OpenAI announced a multi-year, strategic partnership that provides AWS’s world-class infrastructure to run and scale OpenAI’s core artificial intelligence (AI) workloads starting immediately.\nL31: Under this new $38 billion agreement, which will have continued growth over the next seven years, OpenAI is accessing AWS compute comprising hundreds of thousands of state-of-the-art NVIDIA GPUs, with the ability to expand to tens of millions of CPUs to rapidly scale agentic workloads. AWS has unusual experience running large-scale AI infrastructure securely, reliably, and at scale–with clusters topping 500K chips.\nL32: AWS's leadership in cloud infrastructure combined with OpenAI's pioneering advancements in generative AI will help millions of users continue to get value from ChatGPT.\nL33: The rapid advancement of AI technology has created unprecedented demand for computing power. As frontier model providers seek to push their models to new heights of intelligence, they are increasingly turning to AWS due to the performance, scale, and security they can achieve. OpenAI will immediately start utilizing AWS compute as part of this partnership, with all capacity targeted to be deployed before the end of 2026, and the ability to expand further into 2027 and beyond.\nL34: The infrastructure deployment that AWS is building for OpenAI features a sophisticated architectural design optimized for maximum AI processing efficiency and performance. Clustering the NVIDIA GPUs—both GB200s and GB300s—via Amazon EC2 UltraServers on the same network enables low-latency performance across interconnected systems, allowing OpenAI to efficiently run workloads with optimal performance.\nL35: The clusters are designed to support various workloads, from serving inference for ChatGPT to training next generation models, with the flexibility to adapt to OpenAI's evolving needs.\nL36: “Scaling frontier AI requires massive, reliable compute,\" said OpenAI co-founder and CEO Sam Altman. “Our partnership with AWS strengthens the broad compute ecosystem that will power this next era and bring advanced AI to everyone.”\nL37: “As OpenAI continues to push the boundaries of what’s possible, AWS's best-in-class infrastructure will serve as a backbone for their AI ambitions,” said Matt Garman, CEO of AWS. “The breadth and immediate availability of optimized compute demonstrates why AWS is uniquely positioned to support OpenAI's vast AI workloads.\"\nL38: This news continues the companies’ work together to provide cutting-edge AI technology to benefit organizations worldwide. Earlier this year, OpenAI open weight foundation models became available on Amazon Bedrock, bringing these additional model options to millions of customers on AWS.\nL39: OpenAI has quickly become one of the most popular publicly available model providers in Amazon Bedrock with thousands of customers—including Bystreet, Comscore, Peloton, Thomson Reuters, Triomics, and Verana Health—working with their models for agentic workflows, coding, scientific analysis, mathematical problem-solving, and more.\nL40: To get started with OpenAI’s open weight models in Amazon Bedrock, visit: cite4†aws.amazon.com/bedrock/openai⁠(opens in a new window)†aws.amazon.com L41: \nL42:   * cite5†2025 L43: ## Author\nL44: \nL45: OpenAI\nL46: ## Keep reading\nL47: \nL48: cite6†View all L49: \nL50: cite7†How Albertsons Companies is reimagining retail from the inside out CompanyOct 1, 2026 L51: \nL52: cite8†DevDay 2026 Recap CompanySep 29, 2026 L53: \nL54: cite9†Introducing dots ProductSep 29, 2026 L55: \nL56: Research\nL57: \nL58:   * cite10†Research Index L59:   * cite11†Research Overview L60:   * cite12†Economic Research L61: \nL62: Latest Advancements\nL63: \nL64:   * cite13†GPT-6.1 Sol L65:   * cite14†GPT-6 Astra L66:   * cite15†GPT-5.6 L67:   * cite16†GPT-5.5 L68: \nL69: Safety\nL70:   * cite17†Safety Approach L71:   * cite18†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L72:   * cite19†Security & Privacy L73:   * cite20†Trust & Transparency L74: \nL75: Products\nL76:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L77:   * cite21†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L78:   * cite22†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L79:   * cite23†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L80:   * cite24†Codex†chatgpt.com L81:   * cite25†Dots(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L82:   * cite26†Release Notes L83: \nL84: API Platform\nL85: \nL86:   * cite27†Overview L87:   * cite28†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L88:   * cite29†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L89: \nL90: Business\nL91:   * cite30†Overview L92:   * cite31†Solutions L93:   * cite32†Resources L94:   * cite33†Plugins L95:   * cite34†Customer Stories L96:   * cite35†Partner Network L97:   * cite36†Contact Sales L98: \nL99: Developers\nL100: \nL101:   * cite37†Apps SDK(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L102:   * cite38†Open Models L103:   * cite39†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L104: 